  "source": "JARL Amateur Band Plan, in force 17 July 2025. jarl.org. 2 m, 70 cm and 23 cm: JARL Amateur Band Plan, in force 17 July 2025 (bandplan20250717.pdf), jarl.org. Receiver bands (not amateur): summarised from the ITU Radio Regulations Article 5 table of allocations and general knowledge of national use. Broad, and not specific to a licence.",
  "note": "160 m and 80 m are broken up: nothing between the blocks is available. Voice on 160 m is only allowed in 1845-1875 kHz, and 1907.5-1912.5 kHz is 500 Hz maximum with no voice. 30 m is 2 kHz maximum, with no SSB or AM. Power and class limits are in your licence. On VHF and UHF: JARL states its plan may be worded differently from the Ministry's official usage notice. 144.00-144.20 MHz may carry narrowband EME; 144.30-144.50 MHz allows wideband only for contacts with the ISS. Digital calling is 145.30 and 433.30 MHz; the plan lists none on 1200 MHz. Calling frequencies are also emergency frequencies. FT8 is from WSJT-X defaults on 2 m; none is shown on 70 or 23 cm, where the WSJT-X defaults fall outside the narrowband segments. The non-amateur bands are only offered on a receiver, and a guide to what's where, not a statement of who may use it.",
